# 16. Document Relationships

## Purpose

The Document Relationships section defines how the Project Charter relates to other engineering documents within the Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture (AVIA) project.

It establishes the hierarchy of engineering documentation, ensuring that every major document has a clear purpose, authority, and traceable relationship to the others.

---

## Engineering Document Hierarchy

The Project Charter serves as the highest-level engineering governance document for the AVIA project.

Engineering documents shall be developed in the following hierarchical relationship:

```text
Project Charter
        │
        ▼
System Objective Specification (SOS)
        │
        ▼
System Requirements Specification (SRS)
        │
        ▼
Engineering Architectures
        │
        ├── Functional Architecture
        ├── Physics Architecture
        ├── Mathematical Architecture
        ├── Mechanical Architecture
        ├── Electrical Architecture
        ├── Control Architecture
        └── Decision Architecture
        │
        ▼
Engineering Analysis
        │
        ▼
Simulation
        │
        ▼
Verification & Validation
```

---

## Document Authority

Each engineering document derives its authority from the document immediately above it within the hierarchy.

Accordingly:

- The Project Charter authorizes the project.
- The System Objective Specification defines what the system must achieve.
- The System Requirements Specification defines what the system must satisfy.
- Engineering Architectures define how the system is organized.
- Engineering Analyses evaluate engineering concepts.
- Simulation, Verification, and Validation provide objective evidence of engineering performance.

---

## Traceability

Engineering information shall remain traceable throughout the documentation hierarchy.

Changes to higher-level documents may require review of dependent documents to maintain consistency, alignment, and engineering integrity.

---

## Engineering Perspective

The engineering documentation hierarchy ensures that every engineering activity can be traced from implementation and validation back through architectures, requirements, objectives, and ultimately to the Project Charter.

This hierarchy promotes consistency, transparency, disciplined engineering, and controlled project evolution.
